[#:seon.task{:name "a-slow-reply-names-nothing"
             :plan "The SCI reply's `:seon.repl/ms` is the arm's evaluation time only (repl.clj ~218-219): acquisition (339 ms on a probe, recorded as `:seon.eval/acquisition-ms` but not shown), the install gate's disposable branch, and settlement are absent, and no armed function is named. pid 66629's 82 s evaluations left nothing on the cluster (max recorded duration 471 ms). Record [sym ms] of armed calls over slow-ms into the evaluating kernel arm (already per evaluation and carried across threads, adopt-arm), settle them beside acquisition-ms, and show the whole wall time and those names when it exceeds one second; the JVM-wide window it replaced is gone. Done when a regression whose evaluation waits over one second on an armed call gets a reply naming that call and the whole wall time, and concurrent work in other arms is not named. Read `seon.repl/response-entries`, `seon.sci.kernel/adopt-arm` and `docs/research/agent-platform/perf-audit-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "every-reply-repeats-open-faults"
             :plan "Each SCI reply's :errors repeated the agent's same open fault (`seon.schema.datahike/decode-vectors`, occurrence count 1..17) on every evaluation for the whole session, about 150 tokens per reply, whether or not the evaluation caused it, and after the fault's last occurrence. Show a fault in the reply of the evaluation that produced it or once when it opens; later replies can name the open count. More evidence (default, 2026-10-08): seon.cluster.registry/retire-branch! \"Connection has been released.\" (layer :seon.error/normalization, no task) from an unrelated operation appeared twice on a (keys result/...) reply. Also see seon.test/test-release-closes-branch-connections-out-of-order. Read `seon.repl/response` and `docs/research/agent-platform/repl-dogfood-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-slow-reply-names-counts-not-proportionality"
             :plan "A reply over one second lists :repeated [[sym calls ms] ...] sorted by call count (request-merge!: `seon.schema.datahike/logical-attribute?` 199,406 calls first) but not what the work was proportional to or which armed call held the wall time, so the agent cannot tell a hub traversal from a per-member recording. Order by inclusive time, name the top call's input size (members, datoms, program entities), and state the ratio to the evaluation's own input. Read `seon.profile/summary`, `seon.repl/response-entries` and `docs/research/agent-platform/dogfood-loop-2026-09-28.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "offered-operations-cannot-name-the-prior-result"
             :plan "*1, *2 and *3 are nil at the SCI REPL (dogfood loop 2026-09-28), so a value that offers its next call as data cannot refer to itself: `seon.cluster.source/prepare-merge!` offers (`seon.cluster.source/accept-merge!` {...}) with its whole request literal. Bind *1/*2/*3 (and *e) in the agent's context from its own evaluations, as clojure.main does, or have the reply's printer substitute the result symbol it already prints (result/eN) into offered forms, so an offer is short and runs as shown. Read `seon.repl/response` and `docs/research/agent-platform/dogfood-loop-2026-09-28.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "directory-render-returns-text-the-printer-clips-mid-form"
             :plan "render-directory-ai (src/seon/repl.clj) is dir's `:seon.render/ai` face but returns pr-str text (contract :string), so the value printer charges it as one leaf and clips it by characters: (dir my.message) in a fresh cluster is shown as 3,199 characters ending \"render-a…+646\" inside an unclosed schema map, which no reader can read back (EOF while reading), and its tail rows and `:seon.repl/schemas` are unreachable data. Return the {`:seon.repl/columns` `:seon.repl/rows` `:seon.repl/schemas`} map itself so the printer spreads its one budget over the shape and names each cut with a dig form; the HTML face renders the same map (test-data-assertions lane, 2026-09-28). Read `seon.render.value/render-ai`, `seon.repl/render-directory-ai`, `seon.repl/render-directory-html` and `test/seon/supplied_documentation_test.clj`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-jvm-def-is-told-it-lives-in-sci"
             :plan "A def evaluated in MCP JVM mode (a host Var in a real namespace) carries no note now that `seon.repl/def-note` reads `:seon.eval/private` (the batch's SCI decision, never recorded in JVM mode); it should be told it is the host Var and lasts until the JVM restarts, from a decision recorded by the JVM-mode batch. History: it was told it \"lives only in your SCI context\", which is false for a host Var, and the note was printed beside a def whose evaluation failed (observed 2026-09-28, flow-processes-opus: (def routing (... inst ...)) with inst unresolved returned the compile error and the persistence note). Decide the note from the evaluation's surface (SCI vs JVM) and its outcome, not from the text: no note on a failed def; a JVM def names the host Var and that it lasts until the JVM restarts. Read `seon.repl/def-note` and `seon.repl/text`."}
