 #:seon.task{:name "printed-output-reaches-the-reply-unbounded"
             :plan "A form's printed output reaches the reply whole: eval_clj sci '(dotimes [i 300000] (println i))' answered a 2,289,210-character MCP reply (:out-tokens 621528) on default 2026-10-08, while a 2,000,000-character string VALUE answered 5,172 characters. seon.repl's response composes `:seon.repl/out` with `seon.print/string-literal`, a second printer outside the value printer's budget; the only response is a :large-output finding beside the flood. Correct: :out is shown through the value printer's one budget with a dig form for the rest, like any value. Proof: a deftest asserting the reply of 300,000 printed lines is under the value printer's bound and names a dig form. Read `seon.repl/response` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/repl-audit/repl-audit-opus-audit.md`."}

 #:seon.task{:name "the-out-elision-form-re-pulls-the-database"
             :plan "A cut :out prints (subs (`:seon.cluster.eval/output` (`seon.db/pull` '[`:seon.cluster.eval/output`] [`:seon.cluster.eval/id` \"29ea417f846d\"])) 4669) (default 2026-10-08): a database read with a Seon attribute name while the live output exists in the runtime. shown-output hands the printer a `seon.db/pull` requery id. Owner ruling 2026-10-08: elision forms are plain clojure.core on the live result symbol, with no database re-pull while the live value exists. Done when the form is (subs (:out result/e…) n), or (subs out/e… n) on the live output, asserted by evaluating it through the MCP entrance and comparing with (subs output n). Read `seon.repl/shown-output` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/printer-rules/printer-rules-opus-printer-rules.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "reply-lacks-gc-commits-and-a-dialled-allocation-bound"
             :plan "Each SCI reply now shows :seon.repl/alloc-mb from :seon.eval/allocated-bytes (every thread's allocation over the evaluation's window, since SCI runs on a virtual thread) and a :large-allocation finding past :seon.config.eval/bounds (400 MB). Missing: the evaluation's GC count and collection-time delta and the commits its request wrote, which need new :seon.eval attributes carried by seon.sci.eval's evaluation record and seon.turn's settlement and pull list (held files when this was filed); and the bound is declared data in the bounds schema, not a :seon.config/dial an operator can size for the machine, because seon.repl/findings judges the emission alone and receives no configuration. Done when a reply that collected garbage shows its GC delta, an eval request shows its commit count, and changing a dial changes the :large-allocation threshold without a reload. Read `seon.profile/measure`, `seon.repl/findings` and `seon.sci.kernel/arm`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-request-spends-seconds-outside-its-named-stages"
             :plan "`:seon.repl/request` `:seon.cost/ms` exceeds the sum of its `:seon.cost/stages` by seconds, naming nothing: `(doc my.edit/exact!)` on agent repl-only-2 took 10,594 ms with stages summing about 290 ms; a defn plus `my.program/ns-unmap!` request 33,137 ms against about 12,000 ms of stages; `my.program/write-branch!` 8,028 ms against about 750 ms (default 35e95e7b3, 2026-10-09). Done when every request's stages account for its wall time within a stated tolerance, the remainder named as a stage. Read `seon.repl/findings` and `seon.turn/step`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false"
             :plan "`seon.repl/token-boundary?`, the private predicate `seon.repl/syntax-tokens` asks at every character, answers the character itself or nil instead of true or false: its body is `(or (Character/isWhitespace character) (some #{character} \"()[]{}\\\";,`~@^'\"))`, so `(token-boundary? \\()` answers `\\(` and `(token-boundary? \\a)` answers nil (observed in the JVM on default 6299bcda4: each of the fifteen delimiters answers itself, whitespace answers true, and \\a, \\1 and \\\\ answer nil). It is uncontracted. A token boundary is exactly a whitespace character (Character/isWhitespace) or one of the fifteen delimiters ( ) [ ] { } \" ; , ` ~ @ ^ ' and for those it answers true and for every other character false, so `seon.repl/syntax-tokens` answers exactly the tokens it answers today, which `seon.repl-test/colouring-preserves-every-character-including-malformed-source` proves. Its contract names the existing JVM predicate `clojure.core/char?` in a `[:fn …]` with an :error/message and bounded :gen/elements, output `:boolean`; the property generates characters with `clojure.test.check.generators/char` and checks them with `clojure.test.check/quick-check` under `seon.test-support/assert-check!`. Read `seon.repl/token-boundary?`, `seon.repl/syntax-tokens` and `seon.repl-test/colouring-preserves-every-character-including-malformed-source`."
             :children #{[:seon.task/sym seon.repl/prove-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false]
                         [:seon.task/sym seon.repl/implement-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false]}}
 #:seon.task{:name "prove-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false"
             :step :prove
             :product #{seon.repl-test/token-boundary-is-true-exactly-for-whitespace-and-the-fifteen-delimiters
                        seon.repl-test/every-token-boundary-answer-is-a-boolean}
             :instructions "In `seon.repl-test`, beside `seon.repl-test/colouring-preserves-every-character-including-malformed-source`, add two deftests, the step's declared product. Call the private predicate through its Var, `(#'seon.repl/token-boundary? c)`. (1) A table named token-boundary-is-true-exactly-for-whitespace-and-the-fifteen-delimiters, `[character expected]` cases asserted with `clojure.core/=` on `(#'seon.repl/token-boundary? character)`: true for \\( \\) \\[ \\] \\{ \\} \\\" \\; \\, \\` \\~ \\@ \\^ \\' \\space \\newline \\tab, false for \\a \\Z \\1 \\: \\# \\\\ \\- \\_ \\. (the delimiter rows and the false rows are red today, since they answer the character or nil; the whitespace rows are green). Its assertion message is short fixed prose, never a printed value. (2) A property named every-token-boundary-answer-is-a-boolean, written like the existing one with `clojure.test.check/quick-check` 300 and a fixed `:seed` (use 20261013) under `seon.test-support/assert-check!`: for every character c from `clojure.test.check.generators/char`, `(clojure.core/boolean? (#'seon.repl/token-boundary? c))`. Tag both `^{:seon/tasks #{'seon.repl/implement-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false}}`. Run them red on your branch: the table's delimiter and false rows and the property (seed 20261013 fails at its first test) are red; the whitespace rows and the existing colouring test are green."}
 #:seon.task{:name "implement-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false"
             :step :implement
             :needs #{[:seon.task/sym seon.repl/prove-a-token-boundary-answers-true-or-false]}
             :instructions "Make the table and the property green by changing `seon.repl/token-boundary?` only: it answers true for a whitespace character or one of the fifteen delimiters and false otherwise (for example `clojure.core/boolean` around the existing test, or `clojure.core/contains?` on a set of the delimiters), so `seon.repl/syntax-tokens` answers exactly what it answers today and `seon.repl-test/colouring-preserves-every-character-including-malformed-source` stays green. Give it the complete contract `{:malli/schema [:=> [:cat [:fn {:error/message \"should be a character\" :gen/elements [\\a \\( \\space \\;]} clojure.core/char?]] :boolean]}` and a one-line docstring saying what it answers."}
